# The root is a writable ext4. It was a read-only squashfs, which made the board
# a thing that could not be changed: a binary could not be replaced, a key could
# not be added, and the program had nowhere to write the identity it presents to
# the phone. The journal is what a card pulled mid-write costs — a replay of a
# few milliseconds on the next boot — and it is worth it.
#
# `noauto` on the root because the kernel has already mounted it from the command
# line; this line is here for the options, not the mount.
#
# The kernel filesystems below are not optional, and leaving them out is silent:
# no /sys means no /sys/class/net, so a driver that bound perfectly well looks
# like a driver that never loaded; no configfs means no USB device to present to
# the head unit; no devpts means dropbear takes the connection and cannot give it
# a terminal.
/dev/root	/		ext4		rw,noatime,noauto,errors=remount-ro	0 1
proc		/proc		proc		defaults		0 0
sysfs		/sys		sysfs		defaults		0 0
configfs	/sys/kernel/config	configfs	defaults	0 0
devpts		/dev/pts	devpts		gid=5,mode=620		0 0
tmpfs		/dev/shm	tmpfs		mode=1777		0 0
tmpfs		/tmp		tmpfs		mode=1777		0 0
# Logs are memory, not the card. The boot scripts write here, and so does the
# program, and what they leave behind is worth reading only while the boot that
# wrote it is still up: no line of a log is a write to the card.
tmpfs		/var/log	tmpfs		mode=0755		0 0
tmpfs		/run		tmpfs		mode=0755		0 0
